---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/apps/shopify-email";
const title = "Shopify Email, now Shopify Messaging: native campaign ownership";
const desc = "Evaluate Shopify\u2019s native messaging workspace, Flow automations and the service responsible for each send.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <h1>Shopify Email, now Shopify Messaging: native campaign ownership</h1>
    <p>Evaluate Shopify’s native messaging workspace, Flow automations and the service responsible for each send.</p>
    <h2>The product behind the old name</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">current documentation</a>, checked November 25, 2026, calls Shopify Email “Shopify Messaging” and describes email, SMS and WhatsApp campaigns in Shopify admin. It also documents email and SMS automations. The older route is retained here so existing links still reach this evaluation. Do not infer channel access from the old email-only name; check your store’s setup and destination eligibility.</p>
    <h2>Where native operation helps</h2>
    <p>A team already working in Shopify can keep campaign preparation near the catalog and customer records it maintains. The useful question is whether the required audience, content and cancellation rules can be operated there without maintaining a second copy of every decision. Native proximity does not establish that an external SMS service receives the same exclusions.</p>
    <p>Begin with a product announcement: record the collection, eligible customer segment, available stock and offer expiry. Prepare the email and decide whether an SMS adds a separate useful reminder for subscribers permitted on that channel. A campaign brief should name one sender for each message rather than assigning every store a permanent supplier stack.</p>
    <h2>Separate the workspace, Flow and delivery service</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/requirements-considerations">automation requirements</a> specify Flow for full functionality and staff Marketing and Flow permissions. SMS requires channel setup. Two active welcome automations can produce duplicate messages. These documented boundaries make an ownership register useful before activating another flow.</p>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Record before activation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Shopify customer and order data</td><td>Which permission field and order event the audience uses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Messaging campaign or automation</td><td>Channel, content, audience, owner and active status</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flow workflow</td><td>Trigger, conditions, action and failure monitoring owner</td></tr>
      <tr><td>External service, if used</td><td>Connector action, sender, suppression exchange and its own queue</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>A Flow action that calls a service does not turn that service’s delivery or billing into native Shopify Messaging. Inspect the actual action and account. An order notification is also a separate job from a promotional recovery email; inventory both before disabling anything.</p>
    <h2>Proposed activation rehearsal</h2>
    <ol><li>List active welcome and checkout automations in Shopify and connected apps. Select the single owner of each message.</li><li>Use controlled profiles with email-only, SMS-only, both-channel and withdrawn permission states. Inspect eligibility instead of assuming a stored number permits a text.</li><li>Trigger the chosen automation. Purchase or withdraw permission while a message waits; inspect the queue and cancellation outcome.</li><li>Disconnect or disable the sending channel in a controlled setup and confirm who notices the resulting draft or failure state.</li><li>Check destination time, link, offer expiry and current inventory before releasing the audience.</li></ol>
    <p>This is an acceptance exercise, not a reported test result. Keep the previous owner paused only after you can trace the replacement path, and preserve suppression records during cutover.</p>
    <h2>Cost and the reason to add another service</h2>
    <p>The automation documentation distinguishes email allowance usage from region-priced SMS usage. Estimate each channel separately using your actual audience and peak calendar. Add staff time for Flow maintenance and any external service contract to the comparison; a single workspace does not establish the lowest total cost.</p>
    <p>Consider another platform when a required event, audience rule, reporting workflow or destination cannot be demonstrated in the native setup. Compare that missing path with the <a href="/apps/klaviyo">shared-profile evaluation</a> and <a href="/apps/omnisend">combined campaign evaluation</a>. Those are different operating choices, not mandatory upgrades at a revenue threshold. Use the <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">coordination guide</a> to document cross-service handoffs.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
